## Problem
When clicking the "Robot Editor" or "Texture Editor" toggle button, the navigation loses all query parameters (e.g., `project-id`). This causes the user to lose their current project context when switching views.

**File:** `src/components/texture-editor/CommonToolbar.tsx`
**Lines:** 26-30

## Current Code
```tsx
onClick={() => {
  navigate({
    to: ctx.mode === "full" ? "/" : "/texture-editor",
  });
}}
```

## Issue
The `navigate()` call only specifies `to:` without preserving the current search parameters. When user is at `/texture-editor?project-id=123` and clicks "Robot Editor", they end up at `/` without the `project-id`.

## Solution
Use TanStack Router's `search` function to preserve all existing query params. This approach:
- Does NOT hardcode any query param names
- Simply swaps the path while keeping the query string intact
- Future-proof if more query params are added

```tsx
onClick={() => {
  navigate({
    to: ctx.mode === "full" ? "/" : "/texture-editor",
    search: (prev) => prev, // Preserve all existing search params as-is
  });
}}
```

This is the idiomatic TanStack Router pattern for "change path, keep query params".

## Context
- `ctx.mode === "full"` means currently in Texture Editor → navigate to `/`
- `ctx.mode === "basic"` means currently in Robot Editor → navigate to `/texture-editor`
- Both routes use the same `validateProjectSearch` and accept identical search params

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Clicking "Robot Editor" from `/texture-editor?project-id=123` navigates to `/?project-id=123`
- [ ] Clicking "Texture Editor" from `/?project-id=123` navigates to `/texture-editor?project-id=123`
- [ ] Works correctly when no query params are present
- [ ] Any future query params are also preserved without code changes

